{"action": "diagnose", "why": "No tool calls are left. The evidence shows ledger-consumer booked a redelivered event a second time even though the copies were not processed concurrently.", "diagnosis": {"hypothesis": "ledger-consumer's idempotency check let a duplicate copy of a provider event through, so evt_000281 ($157.18, pay_000259) was booked twice. The provider delivered evt_000281 twice, 0.5 s apart. That is normal at-least-once delivery. webhook-receiver queued both copies, at partition 1 offsets 120 and 121. payment-confirmer handled both copies idempotently. ledger-consumer wrote both: for the second copy it went straight to INSERT INTO transactions and then INSERT INTO entries, logging 'posted'. Those writes became transaction ids 281 and 282, which put the ledger $157.18 ahead of the provider. The copies were 0.5 s apart, so this is not a concurrency race. The dedup step either did not catch this event, or the ledger keys duplicates on something other than event_id. Nothing in the database appears to enforce one transaction per event_id either. The ledger did report 17 'already_processed' outcomes in the last hour, so dedup is not broken for every event. Why it misses some duplicates is still open, and so is whether a recent LEDGER_HANDLER deploy caused it: recent_deploys failed twice because Docker did not answer. The only duplicate the money check found is this one.", "evidence": [{"call_id": "c1", "shows": "Two invariants fail. posted_exactly_once: evt_000281 (pay_000259, 15718 cents) was posted twice, as transactions 281 and 282. matches_provider: the ledger is +$157.18 over the provider, all of it on pay_000259. The ledger is still balanced, and our payments and refunds agree with the provider."}, {"call_id": "c3", "shows": "ledger-consumer logged 'posted' for evt_000281 twice, from provider-events/1＠120 at 01:07:27.952 and from ＠121 at 01:07:28.465, with different trace ids."}, {"call_id": "c4", "shows": "webhook-receiver received and queued evt_000281 twice, at offsets 120 and 121, 0.5 s apart. The provider redelivered it."}, {"call_id": "c5", "shows": "payment-confirmer answered 'already confirmed' to both copies. That consumer's idempotency works; only the ledger double-booked."}, {"call_id": "c7", "shows": "In the last 60 minutes the ledger shows 597 posted and only 17 already_processed, while the confirmer shows about 582 already-confirmed. Ledger dedup fires sometimes, not reliably."}, {"call_id": "c8", "shows": "For the second copy (offset 121), ledger-consumer ran an unlabelled DB call, then INSERT transactions and INSERT entries, with outcome 'posted'. No duplicate was detected and no unique-key error occurred."}, {"call_id": "c6", "shows": "recent_deploys was unavailable (Docker timeout), so a recent ledger-consumer deploy could not be confirmed."}], "confidence": 0.55, "proposed_fix": {"kind": "rollback", "target": "ledger-consumer", "summary": "1) If the deploy log shows a recent ledger-consumer deploy (for example a LEDGER_HANDLER change), roll it back to restore the event_id dedup. Check recent_deploys first once Docker responds. If no deploy exists, fix the handler code instead: check transactions for event_id inside the same DB transaction as the inserts, and add a unique constraint on transactions.event_id. 2) Separately, repair the data: reverse or delete duplicate transaction 282 and its entries for evt_000281, then re-run the money check."}, "blast_radius": "Recreating ledger-consumer pauses ledger booking while it restarts. Kafka lag builds briefly and drains after the restart, with no loss because offsets are committed. If the cause is not the latest deploy, the rollback changes nothing and duplicates can recur. A wrong rollback could reintroduce an older bug. The data repair touches the ledger and must remove exactly transaction 282 and its balanced entries.", "rollback_plan": "Redeploy ledger-consumer with the settings it ran before the rollback, as recorded in the deploy log. For the data repair, keep a copy of transaction 282 and its entries so they can be restored.", "fix_action": {"name": "rollback_deploy", "arguments": {"service": "ledger-consumer"}}}}
